# Systematic Debugging (anti-loop)

Applies when a fix doesn't stick, a change "fixes one thing and breaks another," or the
same class of failure recurs. The goal is the **real, complete** root cause — not the next
green run. These are guardrails, not a script: pick the lightest approach that fits.

## Switch to systematic mode early
After **2 failed fix attempts** on the same problem, stop reactive editing and reset:
- State the failing behavior as a falsifiable claim + how you'll measure pass/fail.
- Write down the hypotheses you have NOT yet ruled out.
- Reproduce against real infra/evidence (this project verifies in AWS — see
  `cloud-first-verification.mdc`), not assumptions.

## Keep a change/attempt ledger
For any multi-attempt fix, maintain a short written ledger (in the relevant
`docs/.../*_LOG.md`, the PR/commit body, or a scratch doc) so you never repeat a dead end:

```
| attempt | hypothesis | change made | result (per variant) | conclusion |
```

Track outcomes **per variant** that can fail independently — browser, shard, env, OS,
role, region. A pass on one variant is not "done"; re-check the others before moving on.

## Find ALL root causes, not the first
Rotating / whack-a-mole failures usually mean **stacked root causes** (the Phase 16.38 E2E
flake was 3: CloudFront MIME masking + WAF rate-limit + a UI race). Symptoms shifting from
A to B after a change is the signal to look for a second cause, not to celebrate.

- ❌ Symptomatic: bump a timeout, add a retry, widen a selector so this run goes green.
- ✅ Root cause: explain *why* it failed, *why your fix addresses that cause*, and *why it
  won't regress the variants that previously passed* — then verify all of them.

## Don't claim "fixed" without holistic verification
Re-run the full set of independently-failing variants (all browsers/shards/etc.) and show
the evidence. If one variant is inherently flaky, quarantine it explicitly with an owner +
deadline rather than leaning on retries in the blocking lane.
